data.content:
Done. Here's how it works:

**Production code** — just use `Clock.UtcNow` instead of `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`:

```csharp
public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
    => Clock.UtcNow > dueDate;
```

**Tests** — pin time with a disposable scope:

```csharp
var jan1 = new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero);
using (Clock.Override(jan1))
{
    var calc = new InvoiceCalculator();
    Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(jan1.AddDays(-1)));
    Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(jan1.AddDays(1)));
}
```

**Key trade-offs:**
- `AsyncLocal<T>` ensures parallel tests don't interfere with each other
- Production cost is one null-check per call (essentially free)
- No DI container, no constructor changes, no NuGet packages needed

If you later move to .NET 8+ with DI, you can swap the internals to delegate to `TimeProvider` without changing call sites.
